l research may enable us to grow  replacement body parts experiments in  San Diego in laboratories in San Diego  have already grown a human bladder we  can expect further advances just about a  decade ago scientists mapped the human  genome system we are daily learning more  about how we live and why we die on the  day that mapping was announced I was  lunching with some eminent medical  surgeons at one of Britain's great  teaching hospitals Adam Brooks in  Cambridge and we talked about it what  does this mean I ask the scientists who  were gathered there and it means they  told me that medicine may determine in a  child how that child may die as an adult  and knowing that they can determine the  treatment that may delay that death  significantly the life span they added  for a new baby born in a country with an  advanced medical system that uses the  science that is now available the life  span for that baby may rise from the 79  to 81 years average of today to well  into the 90s consider the social and  other implications of that development  already life expectancy is rising for  four months  in every year it will accelerate now  just a few years ago all of this would  have seemed like black magic very soon  fantasy will become reality in a world  we are only barely beginning to glimpse  now change of course is almost always  two-faced sometimes it brings risk  sometimes it brings danger consider our  earth our blue and green planet it's  altered over the years continents have  drifted oceans have been formed Africa  and South America what's joined together  split asunder species have evolved and  become extinct now these changes  happened over literally hundreds of  millions of years but a few thousand  years ago like everything else in our  modern life it all began to speed up  agriculture began vegetation changed the  growing population began to deplete raw  and rare materials now in the last few  decades we began to emit radio waves  from television radar and