0 and  there are many people in this room who  are going to be around in 2050 though  I'm not at all sure I will be among them  but by 2050 not very far away in  planning terms a world population will  soar to over 9 billion souls this is an  increase from today that is the  equivalent of two more China's  contemplate that these are not just  statistics they're people men women  children with hopes fears and missions  and needs needs for food water clothing  education housing jobs and every basic  of life and while we meet here this  afternoon pretty much one half of the  world goes hungry allege akay today  often without hope how much worse may  the problem be with an additional two  billion to care for ninety-five percent  of whom will be born in countries that  are very poor so planning for this world  needs to begin now politicians are so  often focused on what is immediately  ahead of them because you the electorate  demand that they are apart from their  own wish to be reelected but unless  around the world people begin to lift  their eyes and look at some of these  longer-term trends we are going to be in  very serious trouble when the  projections we can now say  become the reality that is the  day-to-day contemporary problem the  politics and nations begin themselves to  face now let me give you an illustration  of the sort of planning that needs to be  done already many parts of the Middle  East and North Africa face water  shortages water shortages for families  for agriculture and for industry in  North Africa the River Nile has been the  supply of water since the dawn of time  but the demands on the Nile will one day  become unsupportable consider this in  1905 million Sudanese depended upon the  Nile today it is over 42 million and by  2050 it will be a minimum of 50 million  and similar statistics not quite so  stark perhaps apply to every other  country alongside the Nile and the  relevance of this is that action is  required now to ensure adequate water in  30 or 40 years time and if no one begins  to act now the problem will be so great  that it will be too late when water  shortages become immediate and become  acute now these population projections  aren't fantasy they are reality but they  could become a nightmare unless we  prepare for now as population changes so  does the future of our world so does  technology my grandfather who was in  Pittsburgh in the 1860s and 1870s  helping to build the Carnegie steel  works my grandfather never conceived of  the combustion engine let alone  aeroplanes  he never conceived of the telephone let  alone mobiles and now we have rockets in  space and satellites and they arouse  almost no interest among the populace as  a whole so what is life going to be like  for our grandchildren given the change  is accelerating we can't know that we do  know the trend of change technology is  moving ever faster it's getting smaller  and more powerful it's getting cheaper  and more accessible it took the  telephone 50 years to reach one quarter  of the population of America 50 years  it's taken Facebook and Google less than  three years to reach every single corner  of the world now science isn't emotional  it deals in facts 700 years ago it told  as the earth there wasn't spherical and  was not the center of the universe at  the time the Christian Church killed  people for such heresy but science was  right and now science and technology is  the unstoppable motor that is changing  every aspect of our lives consider what  it has done the first integrated circuit  invented by a man called Jack Kilby at  texas instruments in 1958 proved to be  the forerunner of silicon chips  containing literally billions of  microscopic circuit elements nothing no  single invention in the 20th century so  accelerated change it led to the  computer revolution to the digital age  without that crude circuit board there  would be no Silicon Valley no internet  no laptop no Google no ipods no iPads no  blackberries no Playstations and none of  the hundreds of millions of jobs that  they created so who knows what  challenges  head perhaps for example to take one  example of many as a result of the  particle beams circulating in the Large  Hadron Collider at CERN in Switzerland  just last week just a few days ago  scientists captured antimatter for the  very first time although only a few  grains and only for one tenth of one  second but it had never happened before  and this may I emphasize may be a new  frontier it was antimatter you may  recall that powered the USS Enterprise  in Star Trek fantasy is becoming reality  now as science changes how we live  medical science is changing the quality  and the length of our lives 110 years  ago no one knew a blood groups or  hormones or barbiturates since then  medicine has given birth to whole new  industries new technologies are  delivering better drugs healthy food new  pesticides the control of pollution and  advances in forensic medicine technology  and medicine our old partners the first  man to assert that blood circulates and  is pumped from the heart was William  Harvey but it wasn't until the discovery  of the microscope in the 1650s that this  was verified by the determination that  there were tiny connections between  arteries and veins now in a modern  parallel scientists are examining how to  combine computer chip technology with  pharmaceutical research so they can  target drugs to treat specific parts of  the body imagine for example  chemotherapy so targeted to the root of  the problem that it had only minimal  side effects  now science like this is leading to a  revolution in medical care advances in  engineering techniques have given as  insulin pumps for diabetes cochlear  implants for deafness and there are  realistic prospects of repairing nerve  cells for sufferers of Parkinson's and  Alzheimer's disease it may soon be  possible to produce artificial heart  muscle cells and stem cell research stem  cell research may enable us to grow  replacement body parts experiments in  San Diego in laboratories in San Diego  have already grown a human bladder we  can expect further advances just about a  decade ago scientists mapped the human  genome system we are daily learning more  about how we live and why we die on the  day that mapping was announced I was  lunching with some eminent medical  surgeons at one of Britain's great  teaching hospitals Adam Brooks in  Cambridge and we talked about it what  does this mean I ask the scientists who  were gathered there and it means they  told me that medicine may determine in a  child how that child may die as an adult  and knowing that they can determine the  treatment that may delay that death  significantly the life span they added  for a new baby born in a country with an  advanced medical system that uses the  science that is now available the life  span for that baby may rise from the 79  to 81 years average of today to well  into the 90s consider the social and  other implications of that development  already life expectancy is rising for  four months  in every year it will accelerate now  just a few years ago all of this would  have seemed like black magic very soon  fantasy will become reality in a world  we are only barely beginning to glimpse  now change of course is almost always  two-faced sometimes it brings risk  sometimes it brings danger consider our  earth our blue and green planet it's  altered over the years continents have  drifted oceans have been formed Africa  and South America what's joined together  split asunder species have evolved and  become extinct now these changes  happened over literally hundreds of  millions of years but a few thousand  years ago like everything else in our  modern life it all began to speed up  agriculture began vegetation changed the  growing population began to deplete raw  and rare materials now in the last few  decades we began to emit radio waves  from television radar and cell phones  and carbon dioxide emissions have  absolutely sword planes have filled the  skies and missiles satellites and  spacecraft have been propelled beyond  our biosphere into orbit for the first  time in the long lifetime of the earth  our way of life can affect the future of  our planet now this is the defining  challenge for the future can we utilize  the science that is given as these  innovations to protect our way of life  from the effects of those innovations  and once more a single contemporary  example may suffice science tells us  that the risks of climate change are  real Arctic summer ice is disappearing  faster than we thought without it the  sea absorbs the Sun and melts the  permafrost that keeps the lid on  greenhouse gases the burning of fossil  fuels continues to raise dioxides most  not all that most scientists expect this  to lead to global warming of between two  and five degrees doesn't sound much does  it does it matter yes it does because  NASA tells us the when temperatures last  rose two to three percent which they did  35 million years ago sea levels rose by  25 meters even five meters would swamp  large parts of London New York Sydney  Vancouver Mumbai and Tokyo regions of  Florida Louisiana nevermind Bangladesh  and the Netherlands would vanish  Singapore would sink so no wonder green  energy is such a growing business but  here is the dilemma for the policymakers  our modern world is powered by energy  it's indispensable for political and  economic stability a world without  readily available energy is a world that  cannot power industry light homes cook  food drive cars it would be chaotic  without energy we return to life before  the Industrial Revolution it's  unthinkable and it's unimaginable now we  and by we I don't just mean this nation  the developed nations of the world have  been shamefully wasteful of our energy  resources in the last half century and  are now paying heavily for it I referred  to oil at three dollars a barrel it's  now 80 and rising as demand has grown  and grown and it won't slacken demand  cannot slacken as Latin America Africa  and Asia continue to industrialize and  as population continues to grow  so the role of science is going to be  pivotal it must mitigate the impact of  fossil fuel degradation and prepare for  the post fossil fuel age now this is  still many decades ahead of us but one  day it will come already science is far  advanced in effective measures of  capturing and storing carbon before it  leaks into the atmosphere although many  of the worst polluters around the world  haven't got or if they have got don't  utilize the technology that is now  available we're looking at alternative  sources of energy solar energy advanced  biofuels fusion other renewables all of  them have a role to play and so does the  safe expansion of nuclear power probably  a bigger role than any of the others  which an increasing number of nations  not least in the Middle East Sea is  essential to future prosperity and we  also need science to do something that  seems a little more mundane and that is  to finalize the electrical car to reduce  co2 emissions an estimated 100 million  cars are sold every year and there will  next year be an incredible 1 billion  cars on the planet an electric motor can  make a significant difference to the  emission of co2 now challenges like this  are daunting so is the cost but we dare  not ignore them if we take no action my  generation will be fine we won't be  seriously inconvenience but our children  and our grandchildren may face a  disastrous scenario now as we look ahead  there are acute political and social  challenges in nearly every nation in the  world the rising cost of caring for more  elderly people the provision of pensions  for them enrich  sufficient leisure and work to keep  minds occupied and life worthwhile such  challenges will do more than alter the  number and length of our lives they may  change almost everything that is  familiar to us now as we prepare for  what lies ahead it's reassuring to  recall the past challenges that have  been met for most of my life time the  world was at risk at risk because NATO  and the Warsaw Pact were facing one  another with nuclear arsenals that if  unleashed could destroy much of life as  we know it reason and wise politics not  least from past American presidents like  George Bush prevented such a catastrophe  today intercontinental carnage on that  sort of scale is very very very very  unlikely but if the world is safer the  dangers of proliferation of nuclear  weapons of regional conflicts and of  terrorism loom much larger as viable  risks Winston Churchill once referred to  Russia as a riddle wrapped in a mystery  inside an enigma much the same could be  said of Iran today since the flawed  re-election a president Ahmadinejad  there has been widespread internal  dissent by hundreds of thousands of  individual Iranians there had been  marches coded speeches intellectual  dissent as Liberty has raised its voice  and the regime has responded as we might  have expected with repression but like  others before them they will find that  the demand for change for something  better for a free and open society is  impossible to smother forever you cannot  arrest freedom and put it in jail but  the fear of a nuclear Iran is a real  fear  so is the effect of a nuclear on the  evil is not just that they have a weapon  that they might use the evil is that if  Iran obtains a weapon the risk of  proliferation is very very high indeed  Iran worries everyone and destabilizes  much of the Middle East now why is it  wrong doing this the economy is in a  mess the currency is weak inflation  interest rates and unemployment are all  very high it's a very sad come down for  a nation that had a great empire when  the British and Americans still lived in  mud huts now nothing is explicable in  Iran without understanding the nature of  the regime it is nationalist and  hardline and the real source of power is  a supreme leader backed by the  revolution regards the army the  intelligence services and the police the  President Ahmadinejad so often the face  of Iran is in truth a secondary figure  he's not the decisive decision maker too  many people overreact to his provocative  remarks without realizing his  subordinate position he's the apprentice  not the sorcerer the monkey not the  organ grinder so what should we do I  think first we remember the regime is  not the nation as the internal dissent  in Iran makes vividly clear and even so  while the clerical regime survives we  have to deal with it it is possible  negotiations may improve relations and  of course we should continue to try but  because of the threat she poses we must  supplement dialogue with incentives and  sanctions some advocate military  conflict and unattractive though that is  in every conceivable way we cannot  absolutely uninfected but for the time  being sanctions and political pressures  in the  right way forward and we can only hope  that they are sufficient and then of  course in the Middle East there's a  dispute the arab-israeli dispute that  colors the world's view of the Middle  East it spills beyond its own borders  and it has scarred politics for decades  and is it not absurd that we know the  two-state solution we seek but cannot  turn it into reality morality as well as  practicality says that we need a  solution after several decades a  bilateral negotiated settlement is still  far away many wonder whether it will  ever be possible attempts at incremental  agreements confidence-building in the  jargon have failed again and again and  again peace negotiations remember  nothing Ramon remind me of nothing so  much as a mating of the black widow  spider once the dance is over death and  destruction invariably follow how often  have we seen that and how damaging is it  both to Israel and the Palestinians and  the wider region the present situation  is close to stalemate Palestinians are  split secular fat our control the West  Bank Islamic Hamas rule in Gaza Hamas  deny the right of Israel even to exist  they won't renounce violence nor accept  previous agreements made by Palestinian  negotiators meanwhile Israel build  settlements that the world deems illegal  is there a solution of course the  obvious one a negotiated settlement but  if that can't be achieved if negotiators  from Israel and the Palestinians cannot  make the concessions necessary to  compromise because of their internal  politics what then  for how long can the international  community allow this dispute to run on  and on will the time come when the risk  becomes so great that in the wider  interests they have no choice other than  to press more firmly their own ideas for  a settlement the protagonists would hate  this understandably we would if we were  in their position but if both sides take  positions at impede progress what  alternatives exist for the impact of  this dispute spreads more widely than  simply in their own territories will it  be difficult of course it will it's a  gamble a high toss of the coin and one  that will require great political  courage with all concerned but in the  Middle East as much as anywhere and more  importantly than in most parts of the  world statesmanship is essential if the  region is to fulfill its potential  without fear of conflict and if Israel  is remained to remain safe in the  decades that lie ahead now one conflict  is now worldwide it's important to  understand that terror al Qaeda being an  example threatens Muslim societies as  much as in some cases more than  Christian societies al Qaeda hate the  Saudi Arabians more than they loathe the  Americans the British and the Europeans  the Taliban two would change society  back to pre enlightened days with a  rigid approach to religion and civil  life that is abhorrent to most people we  have seen vivid and terrifying examples  of that intolerance recently in both  Iran and Pakistan the concept of stoning  a woman to death or executing another  because she actually had the temerity to  advocate and be proud of her  Christianity is something that to us  seems to come out of an age many  centuries ago that still exists in parts  of the world now this battle against  intolerance and  and unreason is one in which every state  has an interest it isn't a short-term  conflict nor one capable of a clear-cut  finite ending it is too complex too  chaotic for that but it is one from  which no nation can safely exclude  itself we all have an interest in the  outcome for it is our future that will  be scarred unless we find solutions to  these problems let me before we turn to  questions offer one final thought about  the future sometimes in politics and in  life it's necessary to stand back and  take stock now is such a moment our  world has many complexities but more  opportunities if we arrange our affairs  to take advantage of them our future can  be so much more promising than even the  most pessimistic can believe sometimes I  think we should be more aware more  self-confident of what we can do young  men and women today here at this  university will do things see things  know things be things that their parents  and grandparents would never have  dreamed of we must make sure that they  can take advantage of those  opportunities much of what I've had to  say in the last few minutes has been  about science so let me conclude with a  quote from the man Voltaire called the  father of modern science in the preface  to his essays Francis Bacon a  philosopher in Elizabethan England  observed and I quote we have planted  things that are likely to last we have  planted things that are likely to  last so have we in our time planted  things that are likely to last  throughout history change has been an  ally in the future if we are wise it  will continue to be so thank you very  much
 

